### Step 2: Configure Basic Auth with `UserDetailsService`

Set up a `UserDetailsService` for Basic Auth. For simplicity, you can use an in-memory user store, but in a real application, you might use a database.

```java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.User;
import org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.UserDetailsService;
import org.springframework.security.provisioning.InMemoryUserDetailsManager;

@Configuration
public class UserDetailsConfig {

    @Bean
    public UserDetailsService userDetailsService() {
        var user = User.withUsername("user")
                .password("{noop}password") // Use proper password encoder in production
                .roles("USER")
                .build();
        return new InMemoryUserDetailsManager(user);
    }
}
```

